# Target C — Section 124: tooth-pocket transport and the joint full-source packing budget

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only research candidate stacked on canonical audit.46.  
**Dependencies:** canonical Sections 62, 67, 68, 82, 83, 122 and 123.

Canonical Sections 122--123 now give the full-core aggregate source-level frontier

\[
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{1\over D}
\sum_r\bigl(L_r+P_{{\rm exc},r}-2d_r\bigr)
\ge R_0,
\qquad
R_0:={\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3),
\tag{124.1}
\]

and

\[
\sum_rP_{{\rm exc},r}\le10(e_{BB}-k).
\tag{124.2}
\]

This section resolves what the remaining outer-walk term means geometrically. For each harmonic cell, every missing attachment-polygon side cuts off a convex **tooth pocket** whose other boundary is the corresponding unit-contact outer route. The pocket interior contains no nonisolated positive-contact source point. Oler on the route vertices plus the contact-isolated points in the pocket gives a finite transport

\[
\boxed{
G_{\rm tooth}-a_0Z_{\rm tooth}
\ge c_0D_{\rm tooth},
}
\tag{124.3}
\]

where

\[
D_{\rm tooth}:=\sum_r\bigl(L_r-\operatorname{per}(P_r)\bigr),
\qquad
a_0={\sqrt3\over2},\quad c_0={\sqrt3\over4}.
\]

More sharply, each tooth has an exact nonnegative Oler slack `omega_i`, and puncturing all tooth interiors directly out of the **full source hull** gives

\[
\boxed{
\Omega_{\rm tooth}-a_0H_{\rm chord}
\le \mathscr O_A,
}
\tag{124.4}
\]

with

\[
\mathscr O_A
:=\operatorname{area}(K)+c_0\operatorname{per}(K)-a_0(n-1),
\]

\[
H_{\rm chord}:=\sum_i(s_i-1)
=\sum_r\bigl(\operatorname{per}(P_r)-a_r\bigr).
\]

Finally, unloaded-only strict rhombi and tooth pockets have disjoint open interiors. Puncturing both families in the same GWZ complex gives the stronger shared-budget theorem

\[
\boxed{
E_4^0+\Omega_{\rm tooth}-a_0H_{\rm chord}
\le\mathscr O_A.
}
\tag{124.5}
\]

Thus rhombus area defect and tooth Oler slack do not have independent `O(D)` allowances.

Combining (124.1)--(124.3) gives the new source-level three-currency requirement

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{1\over D}
\left[
{G_{\rm tooth}-a_0Z_{\rm tooth}\over c_0}
+\sum_r\bigl(\operatorname{per}(P_r)-2d_r\bigr)
+10(e_{BB}-k)
\right]
\ge R_0.
}
\tag{124.6}
\]

This does not close C-II. It removes arbitrary local outer-walk complexity as a free currency. The promoted aggregate return demand must now be paid by inactive tooth packing surplus, convex cell shape excess, or the single global loaded-contact scalar `e_BB-k`; the nonnegative tooth Oler remainder shares the full-source packing budget with rhombus defect through (124.5).

---

## 124.1 One attachment interval cuts off one convex tooth pocket

Fix one unloaded positive-contact component `I_r`, attachment set

\[
A_r=\{a_1,\ldots,a_{a_r}\}
\]

in cyclic order, and attachment polygon

\[
P_r=\operatorname{conv}(A_r).
\]

Let `H_r` be the canonical induced contact graph on `I_r\cup A_r`, including the loaded-loaded contacts with both endpoints in `A_r`. Let `L_r` be its occurrence-counted outer-face walk length.

Canonical Section 82 adds every missing side of `P_r` and obtains a 2-connected plane graph `\widehat H_r` whose bounded faces are convex. The added sides split only the old outer face.

For consecutive attachments `a_i,a_{i+1}`, put

\[
s_i:=|a_i-a_{i+1}|.
\]

If the polygon side is already a unit contact, its outer route has one edge and

\[
\ell_i=s_i=1.
\tag{124.7}
\]

Assign zero tooth area to this degenerate interval.

Otherwise the added chord `[a_i,a_{i+1}]` bounds one convex face `C_i` of `\widehat H_r`; its other boundary is exactly the old outer-contact route between these consecutive attachments. Let that simple route have `\ell_i` unit edges. Then

\[
\operatorname{per}(C_i)=\ell_i+s_i.
\tag{124.8}
\]

The attachment intervals partition the same occurrence-counted outer walk, and the polygon sides partition `partial P_r`. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\sum_i\ell_i=L_r,
\qquad
\sum_i s_i=\operatorname{per}(P_r).
}
\tag{124.9}
\]

Bridge occurrences are not identified: Section 82's augmentation makes each bounded face boundary simple, while the collection of attachment intervals retains the old occurrence multiplicity in the sum.

---

## 124.2 Tooth interiors contain only contact-isolated source points

Take a nondegenerate tooth pocket `C_i`.

No vertex of `I_r` lies in `int(C_i)` because `C_i` is a face of the augmented graph.

No loaded vertex `b\in B\setminus A_r` lies in `P_r`: every member of `B=Vert(F)` is extreme in the convex force hull, so membership in `conv(A_r)` without belonging to `A_r` would contradict extremality.

If `x` is an unloaded vertex of another component `I_s`, canonical harmonic trapping gives

\[
x\in int(P_s).
\]

If `x\in int(C_i)\subset int(P_r)`, canonical Section 83 would give

\[
int(P_r)\cap int(P_s)\ne\varnothing,
\]

contradicting pairwise cell-interior disjointness.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
A\cap int(C_i)
\text{ consists entirely of contact-isolated source points.}
}
\tag{124.10}
\]

Let `z_i` be their number. Source points on the artificial chord are deliberately excluded from `z_i`; they remain in the full source and this omission only weakens the local Oler estimate.

Distinct tooth pockets in one cell are distinct bounded faces of `\widehat H_r`. Pockets from distinct cells have disjoint interiors by Section 83. Thus all open tooth interiors are pairwise disjoint globally.

---

## 124.3 Oler transports route detour into tooth packing surplus

The `\ell_i+1` route vertices together with the `z_i` interior isolates form a unit-separated set of

\[
N_i=\ell_i+1+z_i
\]

points in the convex pocket `C_i`. Finite Oler gives

\[
a_0(N_i-1)
\le\operatorname{area}(C_i)+c_0\operatorname{per}(C_i).
\tag{124.11}
\]

Using `a_0=2c_0` and (124.8),

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}(C_i)-a_0z_i
\ge c_0(\ell_i-s_i).
}
\tag{124.12}
\]

Define

\[
G_r:=\sum_i\operatorname{area}(C_i),
\qquad
Z_r:=\sum_i z_i.
\]

Summing (124.12) in one cell and using (124.9) gives

\[
G_r-a_0Z_r
\ge c_0\bigl(L_r-\operatorname{per}(P_r)\bigr).
\tag{124.13}
\]

Summing over the pairwise interior-disjoint cells proves (124.3).

Every term is finite and source-preserving; no asymptotic limit or affine-kernel statement is used.

---

## 124.4 Exact tooth Oler slack

For one nondegenerate pocket define

\[
\boxed{
\omega_i
:=\operatorname{area}(C_i)
+c_0(\ell_i+s_i)+a_0
-a_0(\ell_i+1+z_i).
}
\tag{124.14}
\]

This is precisely the finite Oler slack of the selected pocket point set, so `omega_i>=0`. Algebra gives

\[
\boxed{
\omega_i
=\operatorname{area}(C_i)-a_0z_i-c_0(\ell_i-s_i).
}
\tag{124.15}
\]

Put

\[
\Omega_{\rm tooth}:=\sum_i\omega_i,
\qquad
H_{\rm chord}:=\sum_i(s_i-1).
\tag{124.16}
\]

Then the tooth transport has the exact refinement

\[
\boxed{
G_{\rm tooth}-a_0Z_{\rm tooth}
=c_0D_{\rm tooth}+\Omega_{\rm tooth}.
}
\tag{124.17}
\]

Direct contact sides have `s_i=1` and zero tooth contribution. Therefore summing `s_i-1` over the nondegenerate intervals is the same as summing over every attachment-polygon side:

\[
\boxed{
H_{\rm chord}
=\sum_r\bigl(\operatorname{per}(P_r)-a_r\bigr).
}
\tag{124.18}
\]

---

## 124.5 Puncture all tooth pockets out of the full source hull

Let

\[
K=\operatorname{conv}(A).
\]

Choose a straight triangulation with vertex set the full source and respecting every tooth-pocket boundary. No Steiner point is required: pairwise interior-disjoint convex pocket boundaries have no proper crossings; shared/overlapping segments can be subdivided at existing source endpoints, and the remaining source points are inserted by edge or stellar subdivision.

Delete every simplex in every open tooth interior, including its `z_i` interior source vertices, but retain every tooth boundary cycle. Call the result `S^o_{tooth}`.

If `M` is the number of nondegenerate tooth pockets, then

\[
\alpha_0(S^o_{tooth})=n-Z_{\rm tooth},
\tag{124.19}
\]

\[
A(S^o_{tooth})=\operatorname{area}(K)-G_{\rm tooth},
\tag{124.20}
\]

\[
P(S^o_{tooth})
=\operatorname{per}(K)+\sum_i(\ell_i+s_i),
\tag{124.21}
\]

and

\[
\chi(S^o_{tooth})=1-M.
\tag{124.22}
\]

The perimeter formula uses the exact GWZ occurrence convention from canonical Section 68. If a punctured side has a retained 2-face on the other side, its multiplicity rises from zero to one; if both sides are punctured, the final nonbounding edge is counted twice; if the side lies on `partial K`, its multiplicity rises from one to two. Every punctured side occurrence contributes once.

Apply the canonical Euclidean GWZ inequality

\[
a_0\alpha_0(S)
\le A(S)+c_0P(S)+a_0\chi(S).
\tag{124.23}
\]

Substitution yields

\[
\boxed{
a_0(n-1)
\le
\operatorname{area}(K)+c_0\operatorname{per}(K)
-\Omega_{\rm tooth}+a_0H_{\rm chord}.
}
\tag{124.24}
\]

Define the full-source Oler surplus

\[
\mathscr O_A
:=\operatorname{area}(K)+c_0\operatorname{per}(K)-a_0(n-1)
\ge0.
\]

Equation (124.24) is exactly the full-source bound (124.4).

For a global minimum-diameter source, the canonical strict translated-lattice comparison

\[
a_0(n-1)>{\pi D^2\over4}
\]

gives the sharper form

\[
\boxed{
\Omega_{\rm tooth}-a_0H_{\rm chord}+\delta_K
<c_0\operatorname{per}(K)
\le c_0\pi D,
}
\tag{124.25}
\]

where `delta_K=pi D^2/4-area(K)`.

---

## 124.6 Rhombus defect and tooth slack share one packing allowance

Let `R_4^0` be the global strict-rhombus contact faces whose four vertices are unloaded, and put

\[
A_4^0
:=\sum_{R\in R_4^0}\operatorname{area}(R),
\qquad
E_4^0
:=\sum_{R\in R_4^0}
\bigl(\operatorname{area}(R)-a_0\bigr).
\tag{124.26}
\]

Every `R in R_4^0` belongs to exactly one unloaded component because its unit-edge cycle is connected. It remains a bounded face of that component's induced harmonic graph. Tooth pockets split only that graph's old outer face. Therefore an unloaded-only rhombus interior is disjoint from every tooth interior in its cell. It is also disjoint from tooth interiors of other cells by Section 83.

Puncture the unloaded-only rhombi and all tooth pockets simultaneously in one full-source GWZ complex. For every rhombus use the canonical Section-68 operation: triangulate by one source-to-source diagonal, delete the two 2-faces and the diagonal, and retain the four unit sides and vertices.

The joint complex has

\[
A=A_K-A_4^0-G_{\rm tooth},
\]

\[
P=P_K+4|R_4^0|+\sum_i(\ell_i+s_i),
\]

\[
\chi=1-|R_4^0|-M,
\qquad
\alpha_0=n-Z_{\rm tooth}.
\]

The rhombus baseline cancels because

\[
A_4^0=a_0|R_4^0|+E_4^0,
\qquad
4c_0=2a_0.
\]

The tooth terms cancel as in (124.24). GWZ therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
a_0(n-1)
\le A_K+c_0P_K
-E_4^0-\Omega_{\rm tooth}+a_0H_{\rm chord}.
}
\tag{124.27}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
E_4^0+\Omega_{\rm tooth}-a_0H_{\rm chord}
\le\mathscr O_A,
}
\tag{124.28}
\]

which is (124.5). Under global minimality,

\[
\boxed{
E_4^0+\Omega_{\rm tooth}-a_0H_{\rm chord}+\delta_K
<c_0P_K.
}
\tag{124.29}
\]

The theorem intentionally excludes loaded-incident rhombi; they are not needed for the exact coupling and their interaction with artificial harmonic-cell chords is not assumed.

---

## 124.7 Compose with canonical Sections 122--123

For every harmonic cell decompose the canonical Section-122 detour as

\[
\begin{aligned}
L_r+P_{{\rm exc},r}-2d_r
={}&
\bigl(L_r-\operatorname{per}(P_r)\bigr)\\
&+\bigl(\operatorname{per}(P_r)-2d_r\bigr)
+P_{{\rm exc},r}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{124.30}
\]

All three terms are nonnegative. The first is the tooth detour. The second is nonnegative because the two boundary arcs between a diameter pair of a convex polygon each have length at least the diameter chord. The third is nonnegative by definition.

Canonical Section 122 gives the lower bound (124.1). Equation (124.3) gives

\[
\sum_r\bigl(L_r-\operatorname{per}(P_r)\bigr)
\le {G_{\rm tooth}-a_0Z_{\rm tooth}\over c_0}.
\tag{124.31}
\]

Canonical Section 123 gives (124.2). Substituting both upper payments into (124.30) and then into (124.1) proves (124.6).

A coarse trichotomy follows: along every Type-II sequence, after subsequence extraction at least one of

\[
\limsup {G_{\rm tooth}-a_0Z_{\rm tooth}\over D}
\ge {c_0R_0\over3},
\tag{124.32}
\]

\[
\limsup {1\over D}
\sum_r\bigl(\operatorname{per}(P_r)-2d_r\bigr)
\ge {R_0\over3},
\tag{124.33}
\]

or

\[
\limsup {e_{BB}-k\over D}
\ge {R_0\over30}
\tag{124.34}
\]

must hold. The exact weighted inequality (124.6) is the main statement.

---

## 124.8 What is genuinely removed, and what remains

Section 124 does not prove an upper bound contradicting (124.6). It does remove two forms of local bookkeeping freedom.

1. A long harmonic outer route cannot be treated as free graph multiplicity. Its excess over the actual attachment polygon creates pairwise disjoint convex source regions and must be paid by full-source packing surplus.
2. The Oler slack in those regions cannot be optimized independently of strict-rhombus area defect. Both spend the same global source-hull allowance.

The remaining full-core obstruction is now the coupled source-level system

\[
D_{\rm tooth},
\qquad
H_{\rm chord},
\qquad
\sum_r(\operatorname{per}(P_r)-2d_r),
\qquad
e_{BB}-k,
\qquad
\mathscr O_A.
\]

A closing theorem can take any of three forms:

- cap the combination in (124.6) below `R_0D` using the full-source Oler/lattice allowance;
- prove that a linear shape/tooth payment admits a same-cardinality strict sub-`D` replacement; or
- show that the loaded-contact payment `e_BB-k` is incompatible with the Woodall diameter topology once the other two currencies are small.

The pure oblique-grid regression remains binding: nearly equilateral rhombi can have arbitrarily small individual area defect, while near-Oler-saturated tooth regions can have arbitrarily small `omega_i`. No uniform positive gap is asserted.

---

## 124.9 Scope

- Every original source point remains in the source. Points are omitted only from auxiliary punctured complexes used to derive inequalities.
- Contact-isolated coordinates are never inserted into the Section-57 affine kernel.
- Boundary isolates on artificial chords are retained and not double counted.
- Repeated outer-walk occurrences are retained by the Section-82 attachment-interval decomposition.
- GWZ nonbounding edges are counted with their canonical multiplicity.
- Loaded-incident rhombi are excluded from the joint puncture theorem.
- Theorem authority is unchanged. Audit.46 remains canonical through Section 123; this Section 124 is proof-only.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural bridge. C-II and Level I remain open.
